(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_botany_sexualreproductioninfloweringplants=[{id:"neet-2026-q115",pn:115,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Microsporogenesis",q:`Arrange the following in the correct developmental sequence related to microsporogenesis:
A. Microspore tetrads
B. Sporogenous tissue
C. Pollen grains
D. Pollen mother cells
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["D, A, C, B","B, D, C, A","B, D, A, C","A, D, C, B"],c:2,s:`Microsporogenesis is the formation of microspores from a pollen mother cell (PMC) by meiosis.
Correct sequence: Sporogenous tissue (B) \u2192 Pollen mother cell (D) \u2192 Microspore tetrads (A) \u2192 Pollen grains (C).`},{id:"neet-2026-q122",pn:122,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Double fertilisation and endosperm",q:"Which one of the following is a triploid cell?",o:["Central cell","Primary endosperm cell","Zygote","Synergid"],c:1,s:`Synergid is haploid and zygote is diploid.
The central cell first has two polar nuclei, which fuse just before fertilisation to form a secondary nucleus (2n).
The primary endosperm cell (PEC) is triploid.`},{id:"neet-2026-q134",pn:134,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Types of pollination",q:"Which one of the following types of pollination brings genetically different types of pollen grains to the stigma?",o:["Geitonogamy","Autogamy","Xenogamy","Cleistogamy"],c:2,s:`Transfer of pollen grains from the anther to the stigma of a different plant is called xenogamy.
This is the only type of pollination that brings genetically different pollen grains to the stigma.
Cleistogamous flowers are always autogamous.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q139",pn:139,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Anther structure",q:"How many theca are present in each lobe of a typical bilobed angiosperm anther?",o:["6","8","12","2"],c:3,s:`A typical anther is bilobed.
Each lobe has two theca, so the anther is dithecous.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q144",pn:144,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Seed structure, endosperm",q:"Which of the following plant produces non-albuminous seeds?",o:["Maize","Barley","Pea","Wheat"],c:2,s:`Non-albuminous seeds have no residual endosperm.
The endosperm is completely used up during embryo development.
Example: pea.
Wheat, maize and barley are albuminous seeds.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q153",pn:153,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Endosperm ploidy",q:"If the diploid chromosome number of typical angiosperm is 36, what would be the chromosome number in its endosperm?",o:["36","54","72","18"],c:1,s:`The endosperm is triploid.
2n = 36, so n = 18.
3n = n + n + n
= 18 + 18 + 18
= 54`},{id:"neet-2026r-q156",pn:156,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Embryo sac, synergids",q:"Which of the following in female gametophyte of an angiosperm helps in guiding the pollen tube for fertilizing the eggs?",o:["Synergids","Central cells","Polar nucleus","Antipodals"],c:0,s:"Synergids guide the entry of the pollen tube for fertilizing the egg in a typical angiospermic embryo sac."},{id:"neet-2025-q102",pn:102,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollination by wind and water",q:`Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : Both wind and water pollinated flowers are not very colourful and do not produce nectar.
Reason (R) : The flowers produce enormous amount of pollen grains in wind and water pollinated flowers.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],c:1,s:`Wind and water pollinated flowers are not very colourful and do not produce nectar, because they rely on wind and water to carry their pollen.
They do not need to attract insects, so they did not evolve bright coloured flowers.
Both statements are true, but the reason is not the explanation of the assertion.`},{id:"neet-2025-q127",pn:127,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Embryo sac",q:`Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : A typical unfertilised, angiosperm embryo sac at maturity is 8 nucleate and 7-celled.
Reason (R) : The egg apparatus has 2 polar nuclei.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],c:2,s:`A typical angiosperm embryo sac at maturity is 7-celled and 8 nucleate.
Polar nuclei are below the egg apparatus, in the large central cell.
Three cells are grouped together at the micropylar end and form the egg apparatus.
Hence, A is true but R is false.`},{id:"neet-2025-q153",pn:153,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Anther, tapetum",q:`Given below are two statements : One is labelled as Assertion (A) and other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A) : Cells of the tapetum possess dense cytoplasm and generally have more than one nucleus.
Reason (R) : Presence of more than one nucleus in the tapetum increases the efficiency of nourishing the developing microspore mother cells.
In light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],c:2,s:`Cells of the tapetum have dense cytoplasm and generally more than one nucleus.
The presence of more than one nucleus in tapetal cells increases the efficiency of nourishing the developing pollen grains.
The reason says 'microspore mother cells', which is wrong, so R is false.`},{id:"neet-2025-q154",pn:154,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Female gametophyte",q:"How many meiotic and mitotic divisions need to occur for the development of a mature female gametophyte from the megaspore mother cell in an angiosperm plant?",o:["2 Meiosis and 3 Mitosis","1 Meiosis and 2 Mitosis","1 Meiosis and 3 Mitosis","No Meiosis and 2 Mitosis"],c:2,s:`The mature female gametophyte (embryo sac) forms from the megaspore mother cell.
This needs 1 meiotic division and 3 mitotic divisions.`},{id:"neet-2024-q101",pn:101,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollination in hydrophytes",q:`Identify the set of correct statements :
(A) The flowers of Vallisneria are colourful and produce nectar.
(B) The flower of waterlily are not pollinated by water.
(C) In most of water-pollinated species, the pollen grains are protected from wetting.
(D) Pollen grains of some hydrophytes are long and ribbon like.
(E) In some hydrophytes, the pollen grains are carried passively inside water
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A, C, D and E only","B, C, D and E only","C, D and E only","A, B, C and D only"],c:1,s:`Vallisneria flowers are not colourful and do not produce nectar, so A is wrong.
Waterlily is pollinated by insects or wind, not water, so B is correct.
In water-pollinated species the pollen is protected from wetting by a mucilaginous covering, so C is correct.
In some hydrophytes such as Vallisneria, pollen grains are carried passively by water current, so E is correct.
D is also correct (long, ribbon-like pollen in some hydrophytes).`},{id:"neet-2024-q138",pn:138,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollination types",q:"Identify the correct description about the given figure:",o:["Cleistogamous flowers showing autogamy.","Compact inflorescence showing complete autogamy.","Wind pollinated plant inflorescence showing flowers with well exposed stamens.","Water pollinated flowers showing stamens with mucilaginous covering."],c:2,s:`The figure shows a wind pollinated plant with a compact inflorescence and well exposed stamens.
Because the stamens are exposed, complete autogamy does not occur.`,fig:{w:160,h:210,alt:"Compact spike inflorescence with a dark central axis crowded with small flowers and long stamens hanging out and spreading on both sides.",d:[["p","M80 6 C86 20 92 40 92 70 C92 120 90 160 86 188 L74 188 C70 160 68 120 68 70 C68 40 74 20 80 6Z","F"],["p","M72 30 Q54 32 42 51"],["c",42,53,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 30 Q106 32 118 51"],["c",118,53,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 40 Q49.2 42 34 66.6"],["c",34,68.6,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 40 Q110.8 42 126 66.6"],["c",126,68.6,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 50 Q44.400000000000006 52 26 82.19999999999999"],["c",26,84.19999999999999,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 50 Q115.6 52 134 82.19999999999999"],["c",134,84.19999999999999,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 60 Q54 62 42 81"],["c",42,83,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 60 Q106 62 118 81"],["c",118,83,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 70 Q49.2 72 34 96.6"],["c",34,98.6,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 70 Q110.8 72 126 96.6"],["c",126,98.6,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 80 Q44.400000000000006 82 26 112.19999999999999"],["c",26,114.19999999999999,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 80 Q115.6 82 134 112.19999999999999"],["c",134,114.19999999999999,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 90 Q54 92 42 111"],["c",42,113,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 90 Q106 92 118 111"],["c",118,113,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 100 Q49.2 102 34 126.6"],["c",34,128.6,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 100 Q110.8 102 126 126.6"],["c",126,128.6,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 110 Q44.400000000000006 112 26 142.2"],["c",26,144.2,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 110 Q115.6 112 134 142.2"],["c",134,144.2,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 120 Q54 122 42 141"],["c",42,143,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 120 Q106 122 118 141"],["c",118,143,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 130 Q49.2 132 34 156.6"],["c",34,158.6,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 130 Q110.8 132 126 156.6"],["c",126,158.6,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 140 Q44.400000000000006 142 26 172.2"],["c",26,174.2,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 140 Q115.6 142 134 172.2"],["c",134,174.2,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 150 Q54 152 42 171"],["c",42,173,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 150 Q106 152 118 171"],["c",118,173,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 160 Q49.2 162 34 186.6"],["c",34,188.6,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 160 Q110.8 162 126 186.6"],["c",126,188.6,1.8,"F"],["p","M72 170 Q44.400000000000006 172 26 202.2"],["c",26,204.2,1.8,"F"],["p","M88 170 Q115.6 172 134 202.2"],["c",134,204.2,1.8,"F"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q102",pn:102,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Embryo sac and fertilisation",q:"In angiosperm, the haploid, diploid and triploid structures of a fertilized embryo sac sequentially are :",o:["Synergids, antipodals and Polar nuclei","Synergids, Primary endosperm nucleus and zygote","Antipodals, synergids, and primary endosperm nucleus","Synergids, Zygote and Primary endosperm nucleus"],c:3,s:`Synergids are cells of the female gametophyte, so they are haploid.
The zygote forms by fusion of two gametes, so it is diploid.
The primary endosperm nucleus forms when the diploid secondary nucleus fuses with a male gamete, so it is triploid.`},{id:"neet-2023-q125",pn:125,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollination in maize",q:"What is the function of tassels in the corn cob?",o:["To protect seeds","To attract insects","To trap pollen grains","To disperse pollen grains"],c:2,s:`The tassels of the corn cob are the stigma and style.
They wave in the wind and trap pollen grains.`},{id:"neet-2023-q131",pn:131,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollination agents",q:"Large, colourful, fragrant flowers with nectar are seen in",o:["Wind pollinated plants","Insect pollinated plants","Bird pollinated plants","Bat pollinated plants"],c:1,s:`Large, colourful, fragrant flowers with nectar attract living pollinators such as insects.
So they are seen in insect pollinated plants.`},{id:"neet-2022-q113",pn:113,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Cleistogamy",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : Cleistogamous flowers are invariably autogamous
Statement II : Cleistogamy is disadvantageous as there is no chance for cross pollination
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:3,s:`Cleistogamous flowers never open, so autogamy occurs.
No cross pollination is a disadvantage.
Both statements are correct.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q119",pn:119,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Perisperm in seeds",q:"The residual persistent part which forms the perisperm in the seeds of beet is :",o:["Integument","Calyx","Endosperm","Nucellus"],c:3,s:`In perispermic seeds, remains of the nucellus persist.
This persistent nucellus is the perisperm.
Examples are black pepper and beet.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q128",pn:128,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Artificial hybridisation steps",q:`To ensure that only the desired pollens fall on the stigma in artifical hybridization process
(a) the female flower buds of plant producing unisexual flowers need not be bagged.
(b) there is no need to emasculate unisexual flowers of selected female parent
(c) emasculated flowers are to be bagged immediately after cross pollination
(d) emasculated flowers are to be bagged after removal of anthers
(e) bisexual flowers, showing protogyny are never selected for cross
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a), (d) and (e) only","(a), (b) and (c) only","(b), (c) and (d) only","(b), (c) and (e) only"],c:2,s:`In a bisexual flower the steps are emasculation (removing anthers in the bud), bagging, dusting the desired pollen, rebagging and tagging.
Bagging keeps unwanted pollen away.
In a unisexual flower the same steps are followed but emasculation is not needed.
So (b), (c) and (d) are chosen as per the printed key.
Note: Statement (e) was lost from the printed text and is supplied from the options; the printed wording of (c) is kept as printed.`},{id:"neet-2022-q129",pn:129,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollinating agents",q:"Identify the incorrect statement related to Pollination :",o:["Pollination by wind is more common amongst abiotic pollination","Flowers produce foul odours to attract flies and beetles to get pollinated","Moths and butterflies are the most dominant pollinating agents among insects","Pollination by water is quite rare in flowering plants"],c:2,s:`Among animals, insects are the main biotic pollinators.
Bees, not moths and butterflies, are the dominant pollinating agents.
So statement (3) is incorrect.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q134",pn:134,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Embryo sac structure",q:"In general the egg apparatus of embryo sac in angiosperm consists of",o:["One egg cell, two synergids, two antipodal cells, two Polar nuclei","One egg cell, two synergids, three antipodal cells, two Polar nuclei","One egg cell, two synergids, two antipodal cells, three Polar nuclei","One egg cell, three synergids, two antipodal cells, two Polar nuclei"],a:"0",s:`The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.
The egg apparatus is one egg cell and two synergids.
The embryo sac also has three antipodal cells and two polar nuclei in the central cell.
So option (2) matches the usual structure.
Note: The paper prints Ans. [0], which is not a valid option; my working gives option (2). The paper prints no solution; this working is added here.`},{id:"neet-2022-q145",pn:145,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Fruit types, false fruit",q:"Which part of the fruit, labelled in the given figure makes it a false fruit?",o:["B \u2192 Endocarp","C \u2192 Thalamus","D \u2192 Seed","A \u2192 Mesocarp"],c:1,s:`The figure shows a false fruit.
A false fruit develops from other floral parts, such as the thalamus, along with the ovary wall.
So the labelled thalamus (C) makes it a false fruit.`,fig:{w:230,h:225,alt:"Cross-section of a fruit: large outer fleshy region, a scalloped inner ring, and a five-chambered core with seeds; labels A, B, C, D point to the parts.",d:[["p","M37.5,87.5A90 77.5 0 1 1 217.5,87.5A90 77.5 0 1 1 37.5,87.5"],["p","M180.5 94C180.2 95.3 179.7 99.5 178.5 101.9C177.4 104.4 175.4 106.7 173.6 108.7C171.8 110.8 169.5 112.4 167.7 114.2C165.9 116 164.2 117.5 162.8 119.4C161.4 121.3 160.3 123.3 159.1 125.5C157.9 127.7 156.9 130.2 155.4 132.4C153.9 134.7 152.2 137.2 150.1 139C148 140.8 145.4 142.4 142.8 143.2C140.2 144 137.1 144.2 134.3 143.9C131.5 143.6 128.6 142.5 126 141.5C123.4 140.5 121.1 139 118.7 137.9C116.4 136.8 114.2 135.8 111.9 135.1C109.6 134.4 107.3 134 104.7 133.6C102.2 133.2 99.3 133.1 96.6 132.4C93.9 131.8 90.9 131 88.4 129.7C86 128.4 83.5 126.5 81.9 124.4C80.3 122.3 79.2 119.5 78.6 116.9C78 114.3 78.2 111.4 78.4 108.7C78.6 106.1 79.5 103.5 79.8 101C80.1 98.6 80.5 96.3 80.5 94C80.5 91.7 80.1 89.5 79.8 87C79.5 84.6 78.6 82 78.4 79.3C78.2 76.6 78 73.7 78.6 71.1C79.2 68.5 80.3 65.7 81.9 63.6C83.5 61.5 86 59.6 88.4 58.3C90.9 57 93.9 56.3 96.6 55.6C99.3 55 102.2 54.9 104.7 54.4C107.3 54 109.6 53.6 111.9 52.9C114.2 52.2 116.4 51.2 118.7 50.1C121.1 49 123.4 47.5 126 46.5C128.6 45.5 131.5 44.4 134.3 44.1C137.1 43.8 140.2 44 142.8 44.8C145.4 45.6 148 47.2 150.1 49C152.2 50.8 153.9 53.4 155.4 55.6C156.9 57.9 157.9 60.3 159.1 62.5C160.3 64.7 161.4 66.7 162.8 68.6C164.2 70.5 165.9 72 167.7 73.8C169.5 75.6 171.8 77.3 173.6 79.3C175.4 81.4 177.4 83.6 178.5 86.1C179.7 88.6 180.2 92.7 180.5 94"],["p","M126.4 90C127.4 88.5 130.8 84.5 132.6 81C134.4 77.5 136.8 72.7 137.2 69.1C137.6 65.5 135.6 62 134.9 59.5C134.2 57 133.8 54.3 132.8 54.1C131.8 53.9 130.5 56.4 128.9 58.5C127.3 60.6 124.3 63.2 123.4 66.7C122.5 70.2 123.2 75.5 123.7 79.4C124.2 83.3 126 88.2 126.4 90"],["c",130.2,68.9,2,"F"],["c",128.6,77.7,1.6,"F"],["p","M128.5 92.2C130.3 92.7 135.1 94.6 139 95.2C142.9 95.8 148.2 96.8 151.7 96C155.2 95.3 158 92.2 160.1 90.7C162.3 89.2 164.7 88.1 164.6 87.1C164.5 86.1 161.8 85.6 159.3 84.8C156.8 84 153.4 81.9 149.8 82.1C146.2 82.4 141.4 84.6 137.8 86.3C134.3 88 130.1 91.2 128.5 92.2"],["c",149.8,89.2,2,"F"],["c",140.9,90.4,1.6,"F"],["p","M127.1 94.7C127.2 96.5 126.8 101.8 127.4 105.7C128 109.6 128.9 114.9 130.7 118C132.5 121.1 136.1 122.8 138.2 124.4C140.3 126 142.2 128 143.1 127.6C144 127.2 143.6 124.3 143.6 121.7C143.6 119.1 144.7 115.2 143.3 111.9C142 108.6 138.2 104.7 135.5 101.8C132.8 98.9 128.5 95.9 127.1 94.7"],["c",136.5,114.1,2,"F"],["c",132.6,106,1.6,"F"],["p","M124.2 94.2C122.5 94.8 117.4 96.1 113.9 97.9C110.4 99.7 105.6 102.1 103.2 104.8C100.8 107.5 100.4 111.5 99.5 114C98.6 116.5 97.2 118.9 97.9 119.6C98.6 120.3 101.1 119.1 103.6 118.3C106.1 117.5 110.2 117.2 112.9 114.9C115.7 112.6 118.2 107.9 120.1 104.4C122 101 123.5 95.9 124.2 94.2"],["c",108.7,109.2,2,"F"],["c",115.2,102.9,1.6,"F"],["p","M123.8 91.3C122.7 89.9 119.9 85.5 117.1 82.7C114.3 79.9 110.5 76.1 107.2 74.6C103.9 73.1 100 74 97.4 73.9C94.8 73.8 92.1 73.3 91.6 74.2C91.1 75.1 93.1 77.1 94.6 79.2C96.1 81.3 97.6 85 100.6 86.9C103.7 88.8 109 89.9 112.9 90.6C116.8 91.3 122 91.2 123.8 91.3"],["c",104.8,81.2,2,"F"],["c",112.8,85.5,1.6,"F"],["c",126,92.5,3,"F"],["l",34,15,96,40],["l",24,40,124,77.5],["l",25,161,108,108],["l",69,194,102,119],["t",21,14,"C","m"],["t",11,43,"D","m"],["t",14,173,"B","m"],["t",65,212,"A","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q150",pn:150,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Apomixis",q:"Which of the following can be expected if scientists succeed in introducing apomictic gene varieties of crops",o:["There will be segregation of the desired characters only in the progeny","Polyembryony will be seen and each seed will produce many plantlets","Seeds of hybrid plants will show longer dormancy","Farmers can keep on using the seeds produced by the hybrids to raise new crop year after year"],c:3,s:`Apomixis forms seeds without fertilisation, from an unfertilised egg or nucleus.
The seedlings are identical to the parent hybrid.
So farmers can reuse these seeds every year without losing hybrid traits.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q154",pn:154,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Asexual reproduction structures",q:`Match List - I with List - II :
      List \u2013 I                        List - II
      (a) Chlamydomonas               (i) Conidia
      (b) Penicillium                 (ii) Zoospores
      (c) Hydra                       (iii) Gemmules
      (d) Sponge                      (iv) Buds
      Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a) - (iv), (b) - (iii), (c) - (ii), (d) - (i)","(a) - (i), (b) - (iv), (c) - (iii), (d) - (ii)","(a) - (ii), (b) - (i), (c) - (iv), (d) - (iii)","(a) - (iii), (b) - (ii), (c) - (i), (d) - (iv)"],c:2,s:`Different organisms reproduce asexually in different ways.
Chlamydomonas forms zoospores.
Penicillium forms conidia.
Hydra forms buds.
Sponge forms gemmules.
Note: Reproduction in Organisms is not a chapter in the list; nearest chapter used.`},{id:"neet-2021-q113",pn:113,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Types of pollination",q:"The term used for transfer of pollen grains from anthers of one plant to stigma of a different plant which, during pollination, brings genetically different types of pollen grains to stigma, is :",o:["Cleistogamy","Xenogamy","Geitonogamy","Chasmogamy"],c:1,s:`Pollen moving from one plant to the stigma of a different plant is xenogamy; it brings genetically different pollen.
Geitonogamy is between flowers of the same plant.
Cleistogamy and chasmogamy are about closed and open flowers.`},{id:"neet-2021-q131",pn:131,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Embryo sac",q:"A typical angiosperm embryo sac at maturity is:",o:["8-nucleate and 8-celled","8-nucleate and 7-celled","7-nucleate and 8-celled","7-nucleate and 7-celled"],c:1,s:`The mature embryo sac has 7 cells: 3 antipodals, 2 synergids, 1 egg and 1 large central cell.
The central cell has 2 polar nuclei, so the total number of nuclei is 3 + 2 + 1 + 2 = 8.
So it is 8-nucleate and 7-celled.`},{id:"neet-2021-q145",pn:145,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Sexual Reproduction in Flowering Plants",st:"Pollen viability",q:"In some members of which of the following pairs of families, pollen grains retain their viability for months after release?",o:["Rosaceae ; Leguminosae","Poaceae ; Rosaceae","Poaceae ; Leguminosae","Poaceae ; Solanaceae"],c:0,s:`Pollen of some Rosaceae, Leguminosae and Solanaceae stays viable for months.
In Poaceae (cereals) pollen loses viability within about 30 minutes.
So the pair is Rosaceae and Leguminosae.`}]})();
